# Dex Connectors Library

- Keep `sdkgo` and each connector in separate Go modules.
- The first connector directory is its company, matches `metadata.company`, and
  contains `logo.svg`.
- `connector.yaml` owns generated Config, Credentials, definitions, factories,
  branches, UI metadata, and defaults. Never edit generated connector code.
- Every connector has a runnable Flow example with a Worker entrypoint, strict
  FDG 2.0 instructions, deterministic tests, and its static `ConnectionName`.
- Default fixed provider values, derive verified provider-owned fields, and ask
  users only for values that cannot be produced safely. See
  `connector-configuration-ux.mdc`.
- Register each connector in sorted `catalog.yaml`; it is the only shared file
  changed when adding a connector.
- Keep provider operation calls in Step `Execute` and out of RPC handlers.
  Trigger sources may poll or subscribe before routing events to a Flow.
- Preserve durable and released identities and register every Attribute and
  Stream in its consuming Flow schema.
- Keep secrets out of durable state, results, receipts, Streams, logs, generated
  values, UI artifacts, catalogs, and releases.
- Load the installed `dex-connector-contributor` skill through the host's native
  skill mechanism before changing Step, retry, durability, Trigger, or
  Flow-example semantics.

## Code Quality

- Prefer complete domain names. Boolean helpers and internal fields use predicate
  names. Stable public and wire names do not change for style.
- Do not use vague names such as `normalize`, `normalizer`, or `runtime` for
  packages, directories, files, classes, structs, interfaces, fields,
  parameters, variables, or helpers. Name the exact domain behavior, owned
  state, or responsibility.
- Use constructor injection and fail fast on missing required dependencies.
- Lift stateful or reused closures into explicit struct methods.
- Keep one struct's methods in one primary file. Put callers above callees.
- Preserve existing comments. New comments explain non-obvious reasons and stay
  under 20 words unless they document returns or public APIs.
- Document every hand-written public SDK and connector declaration and field;
  skip generated and non-public declarations.
- Handle every error. Add a short reason only for unavoidable best-effort errors.
- Validate untrusted provider, user, and file input. Use named must helpers only
  for trusted invariants.
- Keep the MIT SPDX header on hand-written Go, Python, TypeScript, TSX, CSS, and
  HTML source.

## Tests

- Run all modules with `GOWORK=off` and use the repository Makefile.
- Prefer real Dex integration tests across Worker, Client, persistence, retry,
  Trigger, RPC, Attribute, Stream, and transition boundaries.
- Use unique Flow IDs and convergence polling. Fixed waits are only for behavior
  inside the system under test or elapsed-time assertions.
- Never skip or gate a failure merely to make a suite pass.
